-- title:
--   Evaluated Slice Phase Eight Tail Bound
-- statement:
--   **Moving the second factor to the right register costs at most $\sqrt{\zeta}$.**
--
--   Let $S$ be a symmetric strategy for parameters $(q,m,d)$ with normalized state, let `family` be a slice-indexed polynomial submeasurement family, and let $\zeta$ be an error scale. Assume that, under the uniform evaluated-slice distribution, the state-dependent squared-distance relation between the left and right versions of the evaluated point family at the second point $Q_2$ is bounded by $\zeta$. The phase-seven endpoint `evaluatedSlicePhaseSevenGonnaCite` keeps the second slice factor on the left register, while `evaluatedSlicePhaseEightTailRight` places that factor on the right register. Their averages obey
--
--   $$
--   \left|
--   \mathbb{E}_Q[\mathrm{evaluatedSlicePhaseSevenGonnaCite}(Q)]
--   -\mathbb{E}_Q[\mathrm{evaluatedSlicePhaseEightTailRight}(Q)]
--   \right|
--   \le \sqrt{\zeta}.
--   $$
--
--   This is the postprocessed self-consistency tail move corresponding to paper lines 117--118. It provides the final register-transfer estimate in the scalar commutativity chain.
--
--   Formalization credit: Sirui Lu, Ruixuan Deng, Yanqiao Zhu, and Zhengfeng Ji (arXiv:2609.19814). Mathematical source: Zhengfeng Ji, Anand Natarajan, Thomas Vidick, John Wright, and Henry Yuen (arXiv:2009.12982).
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The source declaration is `MIPStarRE.LDT.Commutativity.evaluatedSlice_phaseEight_tail_bound`; its Lean statement gives the exact quantifier order and all boundary hypotheses.
